Don't let Ctrl-C end time on Windows before its report.
On Unix, time ignores SIGINT and SIGQUIT while it waits for the
command, so an interrupted command is still reported. On Windows,
Ctrl-C ended time along with the command and no report was written.

While waiting, install kill_child_ctrl_handler(), as the exec emulation
and drop already do: time survives Ctrl-C and Ctrl-Break, a child
attached to the console handles them itself, and any other child is
killed with SIGINT.

---
 miscutils/time.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/miscutils/time.c
+++ b/miscutils/time.c
@@ -460,6 +460,10 @@
 	if ((pid=spawn((char **)cmd)) == -1)
 		bb_perror_msg_and_die("can't execute %s", cmd[0]);
 
+	/* Have Ctrl-C end the child but not self */
+	kill_child_ctrl_handler(pid);
+	SetConsoleCtrlHandler(kill_child_ctrl_handler, TRUE);
+
 	resuse_end(pid, resp);
 #endif
 }
